    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter tonight\u2019s slate looking to capitalize on speed and situational matchups, anchored by the day\u2019s premier play, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (79.09). Facing Pallante, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** benefits from an elite combination of a favorable platoon advantage and a high-probability stolen base environment against a pitcher with a slower delivery, making him our primary engine for the scarce SB category. **Caleb Durbin** (65.78) also projects as a strong start; provided he slots into the 5-hole as expected against Mason Barnett, he stands to capitalize on a +10% park factor and a solid SP skill matchup to drive RBI production. While **Lars Nootbaar** (59.86) narrowly missed the \"Strong\" threshold, his +13% SP skill bump against Assad makes him an efficient, high-floor UTIL option to anchor the middle of our order.\n\nDecisions in the infield were dictated by delicate balancing of platoon splits and recent production. **Junior Caminero** (55.92) warrants a start despite the tougher matchup against Winn, as his projected xwOBA remains high enough to justify the slot, even with the suppressed park factors. Conversely, **Chase Meidroth** (44.94) remains a marginal play; while his leadoff spot provides a +10% boost to his efficiency score, he must contend with the significant 14% SP skill penalty against Weathers. We are closely monitoring the status of **Carson Benge** (48.05); while he profiles as a viable play against Eury P\u00e9rez, the 35% rain risk adds a layer of volatility that could force us to pivot toward our deeper bench options if the weather forecast deteriorates before first pitch. Our core stars like **Yordan Alvarez**, **Trea Turner**, **Kyle Schwarber**, and **Christian Yelich** are inactive today due to team off-days, forcing us to lean heavily on the remaining active roster\u2014including secondary depth like **Hyeseong Kim**, **Kazuma Okamoto**, and **Colt Keith**\u2014to maintain our pace in the 5x5 standings.",
